<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · deity</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ningirsu</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ningirsu was a Sumerian warrior god, the patron of the city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/girsu">Girsu</a> and of the state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lagash">Lagash</a>, associated with thunderstorms, floods and the fertility of the fields. The rulers of Lagash presented themselves as his stewards, and his main temple, the Eninnu, was rebuilt by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gudea">Gudea</a>. He was later often identified with Ninurta, the god of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nippur">Nippur</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ningirsu.jpg" alt="Ningirsu" width="320" height="304" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown artist · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Stele_of_Vultures_detail_01_reverse.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The earliest known mention of Ningirsu and his temple in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/girsu">Girsu</a> comes from the Figure aux Plumes, a monument of the Early Dynastic II period (2700–2600 BCE), and he already appears in the early god lists from Fāra and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abu-salabikh">Abu Salabikh</a>. He was regarded as the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/enlil">Enlil</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ninhursag">Ninhursag</a>, though several scholars think an older tradition made him a son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/enki">Enki</a>. His sister Nanshe was the second deity of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lagash">Lagash</a> pantheon, his wife was Bau, and their children included the gods Igalim and Shulshaga.</p><p>Whether Ningirsu and Ninurta began as one god or two is debated. Michael P. Streck notes that they appear separately in the early god lists and suggests two deities merged in the second half of the third millennium BCE, while Thorkild Jacobsen read them as two names of the same god.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>His name means Lord of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/girsu">Girsu</a>. As owner of the land, he was believed to have the mortal ruler administer his domain on his behalf. His common epithet, warrior of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/enlil">Enlil</a>, first appears in the inscriptions of the rulers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lagash">Lagash</a> during their long border war with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umma">Umma</a>, where he is said to appoint them to defend his territory and to intervene on the battlefield in their favour.</p><p>On the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stele-of-the-vultures">Stele of the Vultures</a> he is a massive figure holding a mace and a battle net in which defeated enemies from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umma">Umma</a> are imprisoned, and he stands on a war <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chariot">chariot</a> pulled by lions. His emblem, the Anzû bird over two lions, is rarely seen outside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lagash">Lagash</a>. Ur-Nanshe built several of his temples, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gudea">Gudea</a> rebuilt the E-ninnu with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a>, wood, stone, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a>, gold, carnelian and alabaster brought from outside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>After the collapse of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-dynasty-of-ur">Ur III</a> state the region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lagash">Lagash</a> declined and never recovered its political importance, and the cult of its gods faded. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hammurabi">Hammurabi</a> still provisioned the E-ninnu, and the god appears in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kish">Kish</a> as late as the reign of Samsuditana and in curses on two Middle Babylonian kudurrus.</p><p>His identity was gradually absorbed by Ninurta. Ningirsu is the hero of the Old Babylonian versions of the myths Lugale and the Epic of Anzû, while the Standard Babylonian versions give the role to Ninurta. He also appears in the Lament for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumer">Sumer</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ur">Ur</a> among the tutelary gods forced to leave their cities.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ningirsu” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Lagash state took in three cities: Lagash itself; Girsu, modern Telloh, some 25 km to the north-west, the religious centre with the E-ninnu temple of <b>Ningirsu</b>; and Nina, Tell Zurghul, about 10 km away at the state's southern limit, the three joined by a canal.</blockquote><a href="/empire/lagash">Lagash · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Archaeology keeps adding to what is known: in March 2020 archaeologists announced a 5,000-year-old cultic area at Girsu dedicated to <b>Ningirsu</b>, with more than 300 broken ceremonial cups, bowls and jars and a duck-shaped bronze figurine thought to be dedicated to Nanshe.</blockquote><a href="/religion/mesopotamian-religion">Mesopotamian religion · Legacy</a></li></ul></section>
